{
  "source": "course-assets/critical-thinking/critical-thinking.mp4",
  "duration": 186.4533125,
  "segments": [
    {
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 5.66,
      "text": "Look at the left side. Knowledge forms your baseline. It helps you understand a subject and"
    },
    {
      "start": 5.66,
      "end": 11.06,
      "text": "recognize when something in a text or a prompt doesn't add up. On the right side is the active"
    },
    {
      "start": 11.06,
      "end": 16.64,
      "text": "component. Critical thinking means you question a claim, examine the evidence, and consider"
    },
    {
      "start": 16.64,
      "end": 22.4,
      "text": "alternative explanations before you decide what to do next. When you combine these two halves,"
    },
    {
      "start": 22.96,
      "end": 27.72,
      "text": "the result is better questions and better decisions. Be smarter than the tool."
    },
    {
      "start": 27.72,
      "end": 33.42,
      "text": "It isn't don't trust anything either. It's the habit of asking what would have to be true"
    },
    {
      "start": 33.42,
      "end": 40.36,
      "text": "for a claim to hold up. In 2015, news outlets around the world published stories about a"
    },
    {
      "start": 40.36,
      "end": 46.46,
      "text": "scientific study claiming a delicious new method for losing weight. One front page ran the headline"
    },
    {
      "start": 46.46,
      "end": 57.7,
      "text": "slim by chocolate. The face value reaction was immediate. Sounds great. I believe it. But a"
    },
    {
      "start": 57.7,
      "end": 62.82,
      "text": "what's behind the claim? You must force yourself to pause, especially when a claim"
    },
    {
      "start": 62.82,
      "end": 67.88,
      "text": "matches what you want to believe. The study itself actually existed. However,"
    },
    {
      "start": 68.06,
      "end": 73.68,
      "text": "it was deliberately designed to be scientifically flimsy. This diagram illustrates the primary"
    },
    {
      "start": 73.68,
      "end": 78.9,
      "text": "flaw in the data. The researchers used a tiny sample size of only 15 participants,"
    },
    {
      "start": 79.76,
      "end": 84.58,
      "text": "but they tracked 18 entirely different measurements for each person. With that many"
    },
    {
      "start": 84.58,
      "end": 89.0,
      "text": "measurements in such a small group, a chance result could look like an important discovery."
    },
    {
      "start": 89.96,
      "end": 94.8,
      "text": "The supposed Research Institute backing the study was nothing more than a fabricated website."
    },
    {
      "start": 95.62,
      "end": 101.2,
      "text": "The true author was a journalist. He orchestrated the entire stunt to prove that bad science wrapped"
    },
    {
      "start": 101.2,
      "end": 105.72,
      "text": "in a highly appealing headline will spread globally before anyone verifies the facts."
    },
    {
      "start": 106.54,
      "end": 112.0,
      "text": "Critical thinking requires the ability to read between those lines, particularly when our desires"
    },
    {
      "start": 112.0,
      "end": 118.82,
      "text": "make us vulnerable to confirmation bias. This board outlines five habits you can build to sharpen"
    },
    {
      "start": 118.82,
      "end": 124.92,
      "text": "your thinking. Every single one functions as a direct question. Habit one, is it actually right?"
    },
    {
      "start": 125.6,
      "end": 130.0,
      "text": "You check what evidence supports the claim and verify if that evidence genuinely aligns"
    },
    {
      "start": 130.0,
      "end": 135.7,
      "text": "with the conclusion. Habit two, do I know enough to judge? You have to recognize the limits of"
    },
    {
      "start": 135.7,
      "end": 140.12,
      "text": "your own knowledge and identify exactly what you still need to learn. Habit three,"
    },
    {
      "start": 140.58,
      "end": 145.24,
      "text": "what's missing? You actively hunt for omitted information or alternative explanations."
    },
    {
      "start": 146.22,
      "end": 151.68,
      "text": "Fourth, why am I convinced? Question your own motives to see if you are swayed by hard evidence,"
    },
    {
      "start": 152.2,
      "end": 156.56,
      "text": "confident wording, or wishful thinking. And fifth, what's my call?"
    },
    {
      "start": 157.24,
      "end": 162.14,
      "text": "Finally, you decide what to believe or do, keeping the flexibility to change your mind when you learn"
    },
    {
      "start": 162.14,
      "end": 168.34,
      "text": "more. These five questions work on any information you consume. They are especially critical when"
    },
    {
      "start": 168.34,
      "end": 174.68,
      "text": "you use artificial intelligence. AI can help you find a flaw in your thinking or confidently repeat"
    },
    {
      "start": 174.68,
      "end": 180.74,
      "text": "it. You need to judge which is happening. AI amplifies whatever you bring to it. Good thinking"
    },
    {
      "start": 180.74,
      "end": 182.06,
      "text": "in sharper output."
    }
  ],
